1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,560
Four times a year, a handful of strangers travel

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:06,680
to one of two very boring-looking buildings.

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,980
One is in Culpeper, Virginia.

4
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:11,960
The other is in El Segundo, California.

5
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:13,520
They type in a PIN.

6
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:14,980
They swipe a smartcard.

7
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:17,260
They scan their hand, and then their eye.

8
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:18,660
They open a safe.

9
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:20,840
And inside that safe are the keys to the

10
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:21,286
internet.

11
00:00:21,836 --> 00:00:26,236
Sort of. And in two weeks, for only the

12
00:00:26,236 --> 00:00:28,932
second time in history, they're changing the locks.

13
00:00:30,032 --> 00:00:31,972
You might have seen the headlines.

14
00:00:31,972 --> 00:00:34,532
Meet the seven people who hold the keys to

15
00:00:34,532 --> 00:00:37,372
the internet. It's a fantastic headline.

16
00:00:37,372 --> 00:00:39,892
It is also, according to the people who actually

17
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:41,832
hold the keys, wrong.

18
00:00:41,832 --> 00:00:44,132
So let's figure out what these keys really do,

19
00:00:44,132 --> 00:00:47,012
who has them, and what happens if someone loses

20
00:00:47,012 --> 00:00:47,462
one.

21
00:00:48,012 --> 00:00:50,772
Every time you visit a website, your computer has

22
00:00:50,772 --> 00:00:53,312
to ask where that website lives.

23
00:00:53,312 --> 00:00:56,092
A name like wikipedia dot org means nothing to

24
00:00:56,092 --> 00:00:59,532
a computer. It needs a number, an IP address.

25
00:00:59,532 --> 00:01:02,232
The system that translates one into the other is

26
00:01:02,232 --> 00:01:05,192
called the Domain Name System, or DNS.

27
00:01:05,192 --> 00:01:07,294
It's basically the internet's phone book.

28
00:01:07,844 --> 00:01:10,204
And for a long time, that phone book had

29
00:01:10,204 --> 00:01:13,124
a problem. It trusted whoever answered.

30
00:01:13,124 --> 00:01:14,984
If a hacker could slip in a fake answer

31
00:01:14,984 --> 00:01:18,024
first, your computer would happily believe it, and send

32
00:01:18,024 --> 00:01:20,064
you to a fake version of your bank.

33
00:01:20,064 --> 00:01:22,717
In two thousand and eight, a researcher named Dan

34
00:01:22,717 --> 00:01:25,724
Kaminsky showed that this was much, much easier than

35
00:01:25,724 --> 00:01:26,449
anyone thought.

36
00:01:26,999 --> 00:01:29,559
The fix is called DNSSEC.

37
00:01:29,559 --> 00:01:31,619
Every answer in the phone book now comes with

38
00:01:31,619 --> 00:01:35,699
a cryptographic signature, a kind of tamper-proof seal.

39
00:01:35,699 --> 00:01:38,459
And every level vouches for the level below it.

40
00:01:38,459 --> 00:01:41,819
Wikipedia dot org is vouched for by dot org.

41
00:01:41,819 --> 00:01:44,139
And dot org is vouched for by the very

42
00:01:44,139 --> 00:01:45,959
top of the whole system:

43
00:01:45,959 --> 00:01:46,521
the root.

44
00:01:47,071 --> 00:01:49,711
But who vouches for the root?

45
00:01:49,711 --> 00:01:51,751
Nobody. There's nothing above it.

46
00:01:51,751 --> 00:01:54,471
The root's signature is the one thing the entire

47
00:01:54,471 --> 00:01:56,451
system has to take on faith.

48
00:01:56,451 --> 00:01:59,451
Which means the key that makes that signature had

49
00:01:59,451 --> 00:02:01,319
better be very, very well protected.

50
00:02:01,869 --> 00:02:04,789
That key is called the Key Signing Key.

51
00:02:04,789 --> 00:02:07,919
It lives inside machines called hardware security modules:

52
00:02:07,919 --> 00:02:12,189
tamper-proof boxes designed to wipe themselves if anyone tries

53
00:02:12,189 --> 00:02:13,269
to break in.

54
00:02:13,269 --> 00:02:15,949
There are copies on both coasts, more than four

55
00:02:15,949 --> 00:02:17,849
thousand kilometres apart.

56
00:02:17,849 --> 00:02:19,969
The key never leaves these boxes.

57
00:02:19,969 --> 00:02:20,352
Ever.

58
00:02:20,902 --> 00:02:24,122
But to wake one of these machines up, you

59
00:02:24,122 --> 00:02:28,000
need smartcards. The smartcards are sealed in bags, inside

60
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,682
safe-deposit boxes, inside a safe.

61
00:02:30,682 --> 00:02:33,622
And every safe-deposit box needs two keys.

62
00:02:33,622 --> 00:02:36,722
One is held by ICANN, the organisation that runs

63
00:02:36,722 --> 00:02:39,042
all this. The other is held by a crypto

64
00:02:39,042 --> 00:02:39,637
officer.

65
00:02:40,187 --> 00:02:44,567
There are fourteen crypto officers, seven for each coast.

66
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:47,567
They're volunteers from the internet community, from all over

67
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:49,587
the world, and at least three of them have

68
00:02:49,587 --> 00:02:51,467
to show up for anything to happen.

69
00:02:51,467 --> 00:02:54,189
Then there are seven more people, called recovery key

70
00:02:54,189 --> 00:02:56,487
share holders, who each hold a piece of the

71
00:02:56,487 --> 00:02:59,427
backup. Five of them are needed, but only if

72
00:02:59,427 --> 00:03:01,131
every single machine is destroyed.

73
00:03:01,681 --> 00:03:04,221
So the famous seven people?

74
00:03:04,221 --> 00:03:06,061
It's actually twenty-one people.

75
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:09,371
And not one of them can do anything alone.

76
00:03:10,271 --> 00:03:13,351
The ceremony itself is gloriously over the top.

77
00:03:13,351 --> 00:03:16,571
The officers pass through layer after layer of security.

78
00:03:16,571 --> 00:03:19,711
Two staff members, who must always stay together, enter

79
00:03:19,711 --> 00:03:22,431
a separate safe room to fetch the equipment.

80
00:03:22,431 --> 00:03:25,311
Every tamper-evident bag is checked against a serial number

81
00:03:25,311 --> 00:03:27,051
written down months earlier.

82
00:03:27,051 --> 00:03:29,331
The laptop they use has no battery and no

83
00:03:29,331 --> 00:03:32,011
hard drive. It boots from a DVD.

84
00:03:32,011 --> 00:03:33,931
And every single step is read aloud from a

85
00:03:33,931 --> 00:03:36,447
script, filmed, and streamed live on the internet.

86
00:03:36,997 --> 00:03:41,177
Then, three officers insert their smartcards, the machine wakes

87
00:03:41,177 --> 00:03:43,737
up, and it signs the keys the internet will

88
00:03:43,737 --> 00:03:45,797
use for the next three months.

89
00:03:45,797 --> 00:03:48,117
That's it. That's the whole ritual.

90
00:03:48,117 --> 00:03:50,377
It's designed so that even if one in twenty

91
00:03:50,377 --> 00:03:53,297
participants were dishonest, the odds of a group of

92
00:03:53,297 --> 00:03:55,417
them stealing the key are less than one in

93
00:03:55,417 --> 00:03:55,946
a million.

94
00:03:56,496 --> 00:03:58,596
Of course, even the most secure room on the

95
00:03:58,596 --> 00:04:00,136
internet has a weakness.

96
00:04:00,136 --> 00:04:04,236
In February twenty twenty, during routine maintenance in California,

97
00:04:04,236 --> 00:04:06,336
the lock on one of the safes failed.

98
00:04:06,336 --> 00:04:08,316
Not hacked. Just broken.

99
00:04:08,316 --> 00:04:10,336
The only fix was to call a locksmith and

100
00:04:10,336 --> 00:04:12,636
drill through the safe, which took most of two

101
00:04:12,636 --> 00:04:15,076
days. It was the first time in ten years

102
00:04:15,076 --> 00:04:17,192
that a ceremony had to be rescheduled.

103
00:04:17,742 --> 00:04:21,002
Two months later, a pandemic grounded flights around the

104
00:04:21,002 --> 00:04:24,142
world. So the crypto officers did the only sensible

105
00:04:24,142 --> 00:04:28,602
thing. They mailed their keys, sealed in tamper-evident bags,

106
00:04:28,602 --> 00:04:31,162
and watched the ceremony on a video call.

107
00:04:31,162 --> 00:04:33,782
And just to be safe, the machine signed nine

108
00:04:33,782 --> 00:04:36,214
months of keys at once, instead of three.

109
00:04:36,764 --> 00:04:39,504
So, can these people turn off the internet?

110
00:04:39,504 --> 00:04:42,724
No. The keys only work inside those two buildings.

111
00:04:42,724 --> 00:04:44,564
They only protect one thing:

112
00:04:44,564 --> 00:04:47,044
the signature at the top of the phone book.

113
00:04:47,044 --> 00:04:50,064
And if every key vanished tomorrow, ICANN could buy

114
00:04:50,064 --> 00:04:53,724
a new machine and restore everything from encrypted backups.

115
00:04:53,724 --> 00:04:56,224
Annoying? Very. Apocalyptic?

116
00:04:56,224 --> 00:04:56,599
No.

117
00:04:57,149 --> 00:04:59,729
Which brings us back to changing the locks.

118
00:04:59,729 --> 00:05:02,689
The first root key was created in twenty ten.

119
00:05:02,689 --> 00:05:05,269
It was supposed to be replaced in twenty seventeen,

120
00:05:05,269 --> 00:05:07,569
but that got delayed by a whole year, because

121
00:05:07,569 --> 00:05:09,709
too many systems weren't ready.

122
00:05:09,709 --> 00:05:12,878
The switch finally happened on October the eleventh, twenty

123
00:05:12,878 --> 00:05:16,069
eighteen. A new key was made in twenty twenty-four.

124
00:05:16,069 --> 00:05:19,229
And on October the eleventh, twenty twenty-six, it takes

125
00:05:19,229 --> 00:05:19,662
over.

126
00:05:20,212 --> 00:05:22,472
If everything goes to plan, you won't notice a

127
00:05:22,472 --> 00:05:25,432
thing. Which is exactly the point.

128
00:05:25,432 --> 00:05:27,132
Nobody can switch off the internet.

129
00:05:27,132 --> 00:05:29,572
But four times a year, a few volunteers fly

130
00:05:29,572 --> 00:05:32,712
across the world, scan their eyeballs, and read a

131
00:05:32,712 --> 00:05:35,192
script out loud next to a safe, just so

132
00:05:35,192 --> 00:05:36,932
the rest of us never have to think about

133
00:05:36,932 --> 00:05:37,101
it.
